WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:10.382
Did you know that a crowd
of New Yorkers once filled a hall,

00:00:10.423 --> 00:00:14.928
with thousands more awaiting word outside,

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.055
and that the police had to come

00:00:17.097 --> 00:00:20.225
and forbid the selling
of additional tickets,

00:00:21.351 --> 00:00:25.146
and that the "New York Times"
used the phrase --

00:00:25.188 --> 00:00:27.232
a phrase not yet a cliché --

00:00:27.274 --> 00:00:30.277
that people were "packed like sardines"?

00:00:31.027 --> 00:00:34.114
And did you know that this frenzy

00:00:34.114 --> 00:00:37.367
was all because people
were desperate to watch

00:00:37.409 --> 00:00:41.246
a woman walk in circles on sawdust?

00:00:41.288 --> 00:00:42.664
(Laughter)

00:00:43.456 --> 00:00:45.333
The sport was pedestrianism --

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:49.379
when watching people walk
was America's pastime.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:51.590
(Laughter)

00:00:51.590 --> 00:00:55.969
I won’t get too deep into the story
of our pedestrian, Ada Anderson,

00:00:56.011 --> 00:00:59.723
and the breathless “New York Times”
articles about her walk:

00:01:00.056 --> 00:01:06.730
2,700 quarter miles
in 2,700 consecutive quarter hours.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:10.525
I bring up this long-ago event
for a singular purpose:

00:01:10.525 --> 00:01:15.238
to question what you think you know
about why we watch sports.

00:01:16.364 --> 00:01:20.660
If people lost their minds
watching someone walk in circles,

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:21.703
(Laughter)

00:01:21.703 --> 00:01:25.707
what can this teach us
about the heart of sports?

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.420
Understanding what compels us
to watch sports,

00:01:30.462 --> 00:01:34.674
it's not just a thought experiment,
although it is that,

00:01:34.716 --> 00:01:35.925
but it's more.

00:01:35.925 --> 00:01:37.594
It could have tangible effects.

00:01:38.261 --> 00:01:41.848
If we're willing to inspect
what actually spikes our heart rate

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.142
and pushes us to the edge of our seats,

00:01:44.184 --> 00:01:46.436
instead of just assuming we know,

00:01:46.436 --> 00:01:51.483
we can all, collectively, make
the sports world a more equitable place.

00:01:52.150 --> 00:01:54.611
I didn't say "equal," I said "equitable."

00:01:54.611 --> 00:01:57.489
Fair, evenhanded, just.

00:01:59.074 --> 00:02:02.827
Often, if you ask someone
why they watch sports,

00:02:02.869 --> 00:02:06.122
they will tell you because they want
to watch the best of the best.

00:02:06.623 --> 00:02:08.124
Anything else ...

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:10.877
isn't worth their time.

00:02:11.711 --> 00:02:13.129
This reasoning ...

00:02:14.506 --> 00:02:17.676
it presents itself as biological.

00:02:19.552 --> 00:02:21.930
The men’s sprint champion --

00:02:21.971 --> 00:02:24.849
always a half-second faster
than the women's sprint champion.

00:02:25.517 --> 00:02:27.394
The men of the NBA --

00:02:27.435 --> 00:02:31.731
most of them can jump higher
than the women of the WNBA,

00:02:31.773 --> 00:02:33.191
and on, and on ...

00:02:34.234 --> 00:02:37.445
If we passively accept this premise

00:02:37.487 --> 00:02:43.952
that the only sports worth watching
are those played at the apex of humanity,

00:02:43.952 --> 00:02:48.957
then we have baked into our sports world
a reason for why women's sports don't ...

00:02:49.791 --> 00:02:50.959
and can't ...

00:02:51.501 --> 00:02:52.877
and won't matter.

00:02:54.254 --> 00:02:55.422
For example:

00:02:56.464 --> 00:02:58.425
"Call me when women can dunk"

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.344
is a thing that people have long said.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:05.014
(Laughter)

00:03:05.056 --> 00:03:07.559
Yeah, alright, so we're dunking now.

00:03:07.559 --> 00:03:09.227
(Laughter)

00:03:09.269 --> 00:03:11.229
"Actually, you know what?

00:03:11.229 --> 00:03:15.150
Call me when women can do 360 dunks."

00:03:16.735 --> 00:03:18.695
2050 here, on the line.

00:03:18.737 --> 00:03:22.449
“Yeah, alright.
We’re doing 360 dunks now too.

00:03:23.324 --> 00:03:26.786
Ah, yeah, alright. We’ll call you, then.”

00:03:27.203 --> 00:03:28.329
You get it.

00:03:29.372 --> 00:03:31.750
So let me lay out my theory for you.

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:36.504
My theory about what actually
compels us to watch sports.

00:03:37.297 --> 00:03:41.593
I spent seven years as a columnist
and feature writer at ESPN,

00:03:41.593 --> 00:03:45.805
and I did shows like "Around the Horn,"
"Outside the Lines," "First Take."

00:03:45.847 --> 00:03:48.725
And we would always look
at the sports calendar

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:51.895
and try to pinpoint moments
when women's sports would be big --

00:03:51.936 --> 00:03:53.271
like, really big.

00:03:54.147 --> 00:03:56.733
And it was always the same two events,

00:03:56.775 --> 00:03:58.777
the Olympics, then the World Cup.

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:00.361
Then, the Olympics.

00:04:00.361 --> 00:04:01.988
Toss some tennis in there,

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:04.449
but mostly the Olympics and the World Cup.

00:04:04.949 --> 00:04:09.412
And so we got to thinking:
What is it about these two events?

00:04:09.412 --> 00:04:14.125
What are the common factors that allow
women's sports to transcend

00:04:14.125 --> 00:04:15.460
for these fleeting moments?

00:04:15.502 --> 00:04:17.545
Because even at the Olympics,

00:04:17.587 --> 00:04:19.047
the men's swim times

00:04:19.088 --> 00:04:22.091
are just a fraction of a second
faster than the women's.

00:04:22.592 --> 00:04:27.013
These two events have
two crucial elements in common:

00:04:27.055 --> 00:04:29.808
stakes and storylines.

00:04:30.725 --> 00:04:34.813
This is what burns
at the center of sports.

00:04:36.022 --> 00:04:39.526
In the Olympics, we have all agreed
a gold medal matters.

00:04:40.193 --> 00:04:41.903
Same with the World Cup.

00:04:42.737 --> 00:04:45.824
And now, paired with
these agreed-upon stakes,

00:04:45.865 --> 00:04:48.451
we also have -- at the Olympics
and the World Cup --

00:04:48.493 --> 00:04:50.161
very obvious storylines.

00:04:51.329 --> 00:04:53.998
One person has a USA jersey on,

00:04:53.998 --> 00:04:56.584
someone else has
a different country's jersey on.

00:04:56.584 --> 00:04:57.836
Alright.

00:04:57.877 --> 00:05:01.047
We’ve got agreed-upon stakes,
we’ve got very obvious storylines --

00:05:01.047 --> 00:05:03.341
of course, now, we're going
to have a commitment

00:05:03.383 --> 00:05:04.759
to even deeper storytelling,

00:05:04.759 --> 00:05:08.012
which is how we end up teary-eyed

00:05:08.012 --> 00:05:13.142
after a three-and-a-half-minute
NBC vignette about a Romanian gymnast.

00:05:13.184 --> 00:05:14.435
(Laughter)

00:05:14.978 --> 00:05:19.190
You have everything you need, now,
for the next two hours of competition.

00:05:19.232 --> 00:05:21.693
An Olympic gold medal is on the line.

00:05:22.694 --> 00:05:24.696
And you’ve got your storyline.

00:05:25.071 --> 00:05:28.533
A Romanian gymnast
desperately overcoming the odds.

00:05:28.575 --> 00:05:29.826
Boom.

00:05:29.868 --> 00:05:32.829
Storylines drive up the stakes.

00:05:33.746 --> 00:05:36.708
Higher stakes -- more storylines,

00:05:36.749 --> 00:05:38.501
and on and up we go.

00:05:39.919 --> 00:05:44.340
Every so often,
a female athlete or a team,

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:46.092
they will hit the tipping point.

00:05:46.843 --> 00:05:50.138
We understand their storylines,
we’ve got the stakes.

00:05:50.138 --> 00:05:53.850
Here, I'm thinking Serena Williams,
Simone Biles, Ronda Rousey,

00:05:54.809 --> 00:05:55.977
Megan Rapinoe.

00:05:57.103 --> 00:05:58.271
Right?

00:05:58.271 --> 00:05:59.981
These are the outliers.

00:06:00.899 --> 00:06:03.192
And having representation matters.

00:06:04.277 --> 00:06:06.237
But what tends to happen

00:06:06.279 --> 00:06:08.865
is that these handful of female athletes

00:06:08.907 --> 00:06:12.493
carry the media burden
for all of women's sports.

00:06:12.535 --> 00:06:13.703
Not only that --

00:06:13.745 --> 00:06:16.956
the media gets to say
"Yeah, we checked that box, we're good.

00:06:16.956 --> 00:06:18.583
We did that thing,

00:06:18.625 --> 00:06:22.045
we put so-and-so on the cover,
like, seven years ago.

00:06:22.045 --> 00:06:23.338
We're good."

00:06:23.379 --> 00:06:28.426
What doesn’t happen
is the pursuit of additional storylines,

00:06:28.426 --> 00:06:31.262
expanding the breadth of our storytelling,

00:06:31.262 --> 00:06:34.641
which is what is continually
happening on the men's side.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:39.062
When I worked at ESPN
and I would do a TV show,

00:06:39.103 --> 00:06:40.605
I was in the production room,

00:06:40.647 --> 00:06:43.191
and I was always pitching
women's sports stories.

00:06:43.191 --> 00:06:44.859
It almost never worked.

00:06:45.902 --> 00:06:48.655
Every once in a while, it would work.

00:06:48.655 --> 00:06:51.074
And the thing about doing sports on TV ...

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.745
is that you are not actually an expert
at everything you're talking about.

00:06:55.745 --> 00:06:57.246
(Laughter)

00:06:57.246 --> 00:06:59.582
But you read, you do your homework,

00:06:59.582 --> 00:07:01.584
you figure out a position.

00:07:03.461 --> 00:07:07.090
But this thing would happen
when we would do a women's sports segment:

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:09.133
Many of the other men on the segment ...

00:07:10.301 --> 00:07:11.844
didn’t really do their homework.

00:07:12.971 --> 00:07:14.973
So they were kind of "blah."

00:07:14.973 --> 00:07:17.433
Then, the segment would be kind of "blah."

00:07:17.433 --> 00:07:19.102
There's actually a term for this,

00:07:19.102 --> 00:07:20.895
it's called "gender bland."

00:07:21.813 --> 00:07:23.564
And then, the segment would end,

00:07:23.606 --> 00:07:25.191
and the producer would be like,

00:07:25.233 --> 00:07:27.360
"See? This is why we never
do women's sports,

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:29.028
because they're boring."

00:07:29.779 --> 00:07:33.783
When a men's segment fell flat,
the problem was us.

00:07:33.825 --> 00:07:35.159
We would redo it.

00:07:35.702 --> 00:07:39.205
"Alright, come on, do your homework.
Pick it up. We're doing that again."

00:07:39.247 --> 00:07:41.791
When a segment
on women's sports fell flat,

00:07:41.791 --> 00:07:45.128
the problem was women's sports.

00:07:46.129 --> 00:07:50.717
This is a microcosm
for how this plays out across media.

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:54.971
Never finding the new angle,
never finding the new story.

00:07:54.971 --> 00:07:56.222
So what you do

00:07:56.222 --> 00:07:59.809
is you rely on the millions of stories
that already exist.

00:08:01.144 --> 00:08:02.937
And you know what already exists?

00:08:02.979 --> 00:08:05.565
Every men's sports story imaginable.

00:08:05.565 --> 00:08:07.400
(Laughter)

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:13.448
The reality is that just existing
in our culture,

00:08:13.489 --> 00:08:15.450
you will know a dozen men’s storylines.

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:19.412
I mean, these things are literally
push-notified to your phone.

00:08:20.663 --> 00:08:23.082
Or you're sitting behind someone
on an airplane,

00:08:23.124 --> 00:08:26.252
and you can’t help but see
what they’re watching on ESPN.

00:08:26.252 --> 00:08:29.964
Or you’re at a bar, or a doctor’s office,

00:08:30.006 --> 00:08:33.342
and the talking heads are giving you
the latest NBA drama.

00:08:34.177 --> 00:08:38.473
In America, osmosis will have you knowing
men’s sports storylines.

00:08:38.514 --> 00:08:40.266
(Laughter)

00:08:40.808 --> 00:08:42.560
And by the way,

00:08:42.560 --> 00:08:46.189
when men have a daughter
and they finally get women's sports,

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:48.316
what they're really saying is,

00:08:48.316 --> 00:08:51.986
they finally have a front-row seat
to the stakes and the storylines.

00:08:55.782 --> 00:08:57.617
Let's go back in time again.

00:08:58.409 --> 00:09:00.661
Philadelphia, 1931.

00:09:00.995 --> 00:09:04.457
The Philadelphia Quicksteppers,
an all-Black women's basketball team,

00:09:04.499 --> 00:09:06.834
led by a legend, Inez Patterson.

00:09:07.418 --> 00:09:10.546
Patterson, she knows a thing or two
about storylines.

00:09:10.546 --> 00:09:12.423
She's ahead of her time.

00:09:12.465 --> 00:09:15.051
So she goes to the local
all-Black newspaper,

00:09:15.093 --> 00:09:16.886
"The Philadelphia Tribune,"

00:09:16.886 --> 00:09:19.138
and she proposes a deal.

00:09:19.764 --> 00:09:20.932
Sponsorship.

00:09:21.516 --> 00:09:25.186
Storylines in exchange for naming rights.

00:09:25.895 --> 00:09:30.149
This may very well be the first
naming-rights deal in history.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:34.946
The Philadelphia Quicksteppers
become the Philadelphia Tribunes.

00:09:35.363 --> 00:09:39.784
And I bring this up
because the very next year, 1932,

00:09:39.784 --> 00:09:41.494
there's an intracity rival --

00:09:41.536 --> 00:09:45.623
the Germantown Hornets
with Ora Washington, very famous player.

00:09:46.541 --> 00:09:50.378
And they decide, the two of them --
Germantown, the Tribunes --

00:09:50.378 --> 00:09:54.298
to play a five-game series to determine
the best team in Philadelphia.

00:09:55.007 --> 00:09:58.261
Remember, storylines
was part of the deal.

00:09:58.261 --> 00:10:00.805
Five-game series goes to a fifth game.

00:10:01.389 --> 00:10:07.228
And this is how the "Tribune" writes
about the atmosphere in game five, 1932:

00:10:08.604 --> 00:10:12.650
"The cash customers fanned to fever heat
by the ardor and closeness of combat

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:15.945
gave outlet to all kinds
of riotous impulses."

00:10:15.945 --> 00:10:17.196
Alright?

00:10:17.238 --> 00:10:20.825
This may not sound familiar yet,
but our girl, Ada Anderson?

00:10:20.867 --> 00:10:24.745
1879, the "New York Times"
writes about her walk.

00:10:24.745 --> 00:10:25.997
Her walk.

00:10:25.997 --> 00:10:28.875
"Many women were seen to faint
in the dense crowd,

00:10:28.916 --> 00:10:30.751
but as they could not be carried out,

00:10:30.751 --> 00:10:33.379
it is not known what became of them."

00:10:33.421 --> 00:10:36.382
(Laughter)

00:10:36.382 --> 00:10:40.803
Hot damn, y'all, people
loved ladies' sports, right?

00:10:40.803 --> 00:10:42.138
(Laughter)

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:43.347
I mean ...

00:10:43.389 --> 00:10:45.516
(Applause)

00:10:45.516 --> 00:10:46.684
Who knew?

00:10:47.226 --> 00:10:50.730
Well, wherever you find
compelling storylines

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:52.815
and agreed-upon stakes,

00:10:52.857 --> 00:10:56.110
you will find people
on the edge of their seat.

00:10:56.777 --> 00:10:59.864
No matter the vertical leap
of the participant.

00:11:01.282 --> 00:11:02.408
So ...

00:11:03.075 --> 00:11:06.621
Who tells the storylines
of women’s sports today?

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:11.000
Many of you will know the statistic
I'm about to put up.

00:11:11.459 --> 00:11:14.545
University of Southern California,
Purdue University,

00:11:14.587 --> 00:11:15.963
released in 2021,

00:11:15.963 --> 00:11:20.843
studying the year 2019
in sports media coverage.

00:11:23.137 --> 00:11:27.433
Of all of the minutes
of ESPN's SportsCenter in 2019,

00:11:27.433 --> 00:11:31.020
5.1 percent of them
given to women's sports.

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:35.650
You know what's really sad
about this number?

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:39.612
It's that it's not even
as high as it looks,

00:11:39.654 --> 00:11:42.740
because 2019 was a Women's World Cup year.

00:11:42.782 --> 00:11:44.116
Audience: Woo-hoo.

00:11:44.158 --> 00:11:47.245
KF: What happens when you take out
the Women’s World Cup minutes?

00:11:47.787 --> 00:11:50.665
3.5 percent

00:11:50.706 --> 00:11:52.833
of all of the minutes of SportsCenter ...

00:11:54.168 --> 00:11:55.336
given to women's sports.

00:11:56.921 --> 00:12:01.092
Now what if I told you that number
is even higher than it seems?

00:12:01.759 --> 00:12:04.929
Because what mostly makes up that number

00:12:04.971 --> 00:12:06.806
is, like, a game recap,

00:12:06.806 --> 00:12:08.432
a quick highlight.

00:12:08.432 --> 00:12:11.018
We call these "one-and-dones."

00:12:11.394 --> 00:12:13.104
There is nothing to think about.

00:12:13.104 --> 00:12:14.814
There's no follow-up.

00:12:15.481 --> 00:12:17.441
It is a “storyline” for a reason,

00:12:17.441 --> 00:12:20.152
because a "line" keeps going.

00:12:20.528 --> 00:12:21.696
(Laughter)

00:12:22.321 --> 00:12:24.740
A game recap is not a storyline.

00:12:25.783 --> 00:12:31.497
What drives interest in sports
are the heroes and the villains,

00:12:31.497 --> 00:12:33.207
and the "Were they paid too much?"

00:12:33.207 --> 00:12:35.418
and "Should that coach be fired?"

00:12:36.002 --> 00:12:38.170
Something to really sink your teeth into

00:12:38.170 --> 00:12:39.839
and to stake a position on.

00:12:41.382 --> 00:12:45.219
Do you know that ESPN
has had the rights to the WNBA

00:12:45.261 --> 00:12:48.055
all 26 years of the league's existence?

00:12:48.597 --> 00:12:50.224
And in that time,

00:12:50.224 --> 00:12:57.231
not one studio show was ever made
to introduce people to these storylines

00:12:57.231 --> 00:12:58.816
of the WNBA.

00:13:00.901 --> 00:13:05.323
People will tell you that number
reflects consumer demand.

00:13:06.741 --> 00:13:09.076
But that number reflects one thing --

00:13:10.494 --> 00:13:11.704
that number.

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:15.666
Who can want more of something
they have never been given?

00:13:16.667 --> 00:13:20.588
And how can we believe
we want more of something

00:13:20.588 --> 00:13:26.177
if the reasons for not wanting it
are biological?

00:13:28.512 --> 00:13:30.181
That feels pathological.

00:13:31.932 --> 00:13:33.559
Remember Ada Anderson?

00:13:34.060 --> 00:13:36.562
Remember the Philadelphia Tribunes?

00:13:36.562 --> 00:13:39.982
"The cash customers fanned to fever heat."

00:13:40.649 --> 00:13:43.778
And why did they fan the fever heat?

00:13:44.612 --> 00:13:49.325
Because our collective love of sport
is deep, and it's human,

00:13:49.367 --> 00:13:52.661
and it's driven by deep
and human motivations.

00:13:53.704 --> 00:13:57.208
The physical expression of athleticism

00:13:57.249 --> 00:14:00.544
is just one element of the equation.

00:14:00.586 --> 00:14:02.838
But somehow, for reasons we know,

00:14:02.880 --> 00:14:09.136
it has been elevated above all else
to justify our current model.

00:14:10.554 --> 00:14:12.515
This is a model built ...

00:14:13.099 --> 00:14:15.059
without vision,

00:14:15.101 --> 00:14:20.314
meant to, reluctantly,
provide women a small place.

00:14:21.649 --> 00:14:22.817
But never ...

00:14:23.734 --> 00:14:25.403
not really, a chance.

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:27.488
Thank you.

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:29.990
(Cheers and applause)

